```cpp
void ParseEncodedData(const uint8_t* buffer, size_t buffer_size) {
    if (buffer_size == 0) {
        // Handle error: buffer is empty
        return;
    }

    const uint8_t* ptr = buffer;
    uint64_t value = 0;
    int shift = 0;

    while (true) {
        if (ptr >= buffer + buffer_size) {
            // Handle error: unexpected end of buffer
            return;
        }
        uint8_t byte = *ptr;
        value |= static_cast<uint64_t>(byte & 0x7F) << shift;
        shift += 7;
        if ((byte & 0x80) == 0) {
            break;
        }
        ptr++;
    }

    tail_node_id_ = value;
}
```

In the solution, we iterate through the input buffer, extracting 7 bits at a time and storing them in the `value` variable. The loop continues until a byte with the most significant bit set to 0 is encountered, indicating the end of the variable-length encoding. The parsed value is then stored in the `tail_node_id_` member variable. Error handling for empty buffers and unexpected end of buffer conditions is also included.

