```

Your task is to implement the `variance` function to calculate the variance of the input dataset and ensure that it produces the correct output for the given example.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>

namespace statistics {
    double mean(const std::vector<double>& data) {
        double sum = 0.0;
        for (const auto& value : data) {
            sum += value;
        }
        return sum / data.size();
    }

    double variance(const std::vector<double>& data) {
        double mean_value = mean(data);
        double sum_squared_diff = 0.0;
        for (const auto& value : data) {
            sum_squared_diff += (value - mean_value) * (value - mean_value);
        }
        return sum_squared_diff / data.size();
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<double> num_friends = { 15, 20, 25, 30, 35 };
    std::cout << "Variance: " << statistics::variance(num_friends) << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

The `variance` function is implemented within the `statistics` namespace. It first calculates the mean of the input dataset using the `mean` function. Then, it calculates the sum of squared differences between each value and the mean. Finally, it returns the variance by dividing the sum of squared differences by the number of elements in the dataset. The `main` function demonstrates the usage of the `variance` function with the given example dataset and outputs the variance value.

