struct Node {
    std::vector<Node*> son;
};

std::vector<int> bfsTraversal(Node* root) {
    std::vector<int> result;
    if (root == nullptr) {
        return result;
    }

    std::queue<Node*> q;
    q.push(root);

    while (!q.empty()) {
        Node* current = q.front();
        q.pop();
        result.push_back(current->value); // Assuming the Node structure has a member 'value' representing the node's value

        for (Node* child : current->son) {
            if (child != nullptr) {
                q.push(child);
            }
        }
    }

    return result;
}
```
In this solution, we use a standard queue to perform the BFS traversal of the tree. We start with the root node, enqueue it, and then iterate through the queue, dequeuing nodes and enqueueing their children until the queue is empty. The order in which the nodes are dequeued and processed represents the BFS traversal order, which is stored in the `result` vector and returned at the end.

