public:
    void setOther(SharedPtr<CircularDependencyObject> other);
    void cleanup();
};
```
The `setOther` method is used to set a reference to another `CircularDependencyObject`. The `cleanup` method is used to perform cleanup operations on the object.

Your task is to implement a function `bool hasCircularDependency(SharedPtr<CircularDependencyObject> obj)` that takes a shared pointer to a `CircularDependencyObject` and returns `true` if the object has a circular dependency, and `false` otherwise.

You should assume that the shared pointer implementation (`SharedPtr`) maintains reference counts and handles memory deallocation when the reference count reaches zero.

Write the `hasCircularDependency` function to efficiently detect circular dependencies in the system and return the result.

```cpp
#include <unordered_set>

bool hasCircularDependency(SharedPtr<CircularDependencyObject> obj) {
    std::unordered_set<SharedPtr<CircularDependencyObject>> visited;

    while (obj != nullptr) {
        if (visited.count(obj) > 0) {
            return true;  // Circular dependency detected
        }
        visited.insert(obj);
        obj = obj->getOther();
    }

    return false;  // No circular dependency found
}
