You are tasked with implementing a function that performs automatic differentiation using the forward mode of AD (Automatic Differentiation). The function should take in two matrices and a scalar function, and return the derivative of the function with respect to the input matrices.

You are given the following code snippet as a reference for the test cases and usage of the automatic differentiation function:

```cpp
Eigen::MatrixXd A(2, 2);
A << 2, 3, 3, 7;
Eigen::MatrixXd B(2, 2);
B << 5, 6, 7, 8;
Eigen::MatrixXd D(2, 2);
D << 9, 10, 11, 12;
stan::test::expect_ad(tols, f, D, A, B);

// exception tests
// non-square second arg
Eigen::MatrixXd a34(3, 4);
stan::test::expect_ad(f, c, a34, b);

// non-square first arg
```

Your task is to implement the `expect_ad` function and handle the exception cases for non-square input matrices. The `expect_ad` function should perform automatic differentiation using the forward mode of AD and return the derivative of the function `f` with respect to the input matrices `A` and `B`.

```cpp
#include <Eigen/Dense>

// Function to perform automatic differentiation using the forward mode of AD
Eigen::MatrixXd expect_ad(double tols, double (*f)(Eigen::MatrixXd, Eigen::MatrixXd), Eigen::MatrixXd D, Eigen::MatrixXd A, Eigen::MatrixXd B) {
    // Check if the input matrices A and B are square
    if (A.rows() != A.cols() || B.rows() != B.cols()) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("Input matrices must be square for automatic differentiation.");
    }

    // Perform forward mode automatic differentiation
    Eigen::MatrixXd A_gradient = Eigen::MatrixXd::Zero(A.rows(), A.cols());
    Eigen::MatrixXd B_gradient = Eigen::MatrixXd::Zero(B.rows(), B.cols());

    for (int i = 0; i < A.rows(); i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < A.cols(); j++) {
            Eigen::MatrixXd A_epsilon = Eigen::MatrixXd::Zero(A.rows(), A.cols());
            A_epsilon(i, j) = tols;

            Eigen::MatrixXd B_epsilon = Eigen::MatrixXd::Zero(B.rows(), B.cols());
            B_epsilon(i, j) = tols;

            double f_plus_A = f(A + A_epsilon, B);
            double f_minus_A = f(A - A_epsilon, B);
            double f_plus_B = f(A, B + B_epsilon);
            double f_minus_B = f(A, B - B_epsilon);

            A_gradient(i, j) = (f_plus_A - f_minus_A) / (2 * tols);
            B_gradient(i, j) = (f_plus_B - f_minus_B) / (2 * tols);
        }
    }

    // Return the gradients of the function with respect to A and B
    return A_gradient, B_gradient;
}
```

In the solution, the `expect_ad` function takes in the tolerance `tols`, a scalar function `f`, and the input matrices `D`, `A`, and `B`. It first checks if the input matrices `A` and `B` are square, and then performs forward mode automatic differentiation to calculate the gradients of the function `f` with respect to the input matrices `A` and `B`. If the input matrices are not square, the function throws an `invalid_argument` exception.

