You are tasked with implementing a custom `equal` function that compares two ranges for equality. The `equal` function should take two ranges as input and return `true` if the ranges are equal, i.e., if they have the same elements in the same order, and `false` otherwise. The ranges can be of any type that supports iteration, such as arrays, vectors, or any custom iterable type.

Your task is to implement the `equal` function template, ensuring that it works correctly for different types of ranges and elements. You should also consider the efficiency of your implementation, aiming for a solution with a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of elements in the ranges.

You can assume that the ranges will not be empty and that they will contain elements of the same type.

Your implementation should adhere to the following function signature:
```cpp
template<typename Range1, typename Range2>
bool equal(Range1 const& _range1, Range2 const& _range2);
```

Example:
```cpp
std::vector<int> vec1 = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
std::array<int, 5> arr1 = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
std::list<int> list1 = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
std::vector<int> vec2 = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
std::array<int, 5> arr2 = {1, 2, 3, 4, 6};
std::list<int> list2 = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

bool result1 = equal(vec1, vec2);  // should return true
bool result2 = equal(arr1, arr2);  // should return false
bool result3 = equal(list1, list2);  // should return true
```

```cpp
template<typename Range1, typename Range2>
bool equal(Range1 const& _range1, Range2 const& _range2) {
    auto it1 = std::begin(_range1);
    auto it2 = std::begin(_range2);
    const auto end1 = std::end(_range1);
    const auto end2 = std::end(_range2);

    while (it1 != end1 && it2 != end2) {
        if (*it1 != *it2) {
            return false;
        }
        ++it1;
        ++it2;
    }

    return (it1 == end1) && (it2 == end2);
}
```

The `equal` function template iterates through both ranges simultaneously, comparing the elements at each position. If it finds any mismatch, it returns `false`. If both ranges are exhausted simultaneously, it returns `true`, indicating that the ranges are equal. This implementation has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of elements in the ranges.

