vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
    unordered_map<int, int> numMap;
    vector<int> result;

    for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
        int complement = target - nums[i];
        if (numMap.find(complement) != numMap.end()) {
            result.push_back(numMap[complement]);
            result.push_back(i);
            return result;
        }
        numMap[nums[i]] = i;
    }
    return result;
}
```

The solution uses a hash map to store the elements of the array and their indices. It iterates through the array, calculating the complement for each element with respect to the target. If the complement is found in the hash map, it means that the current element and its complement sum up to the target, and their indices are returned. If no such pair is found, an empty array is returned.

