int strStr(string haystack, string needle) {
    if (needle.empty()) return 0; // Empty needle matches at index 0

    int n = haystack.size(), m = needle.size();
    vector<vector<int>> dfa(256, vector<int>(m + 1, 0));

    // DFA construction
    dfa[needle[0]][0] = 1;
    int lps = 0;
    for (int state = 1; state < m; state++) {
        for (int c = 0; c < 256; c++)
            dfa[c][state] = dfa[c][lps];
        dfa[needle[state]][state] = state + 1;
        lps = dfa[needle[state]][lps];
    }

    // Pattern matching using DFA
    int state = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        state = dfa[haystack[i]][state];
        if (state == m) return i - m + 1; // Pattern found at index i - m + 1
    }

    return -1; // Pattern not found in the text
}
```

The `strStr` function constructs the DFA for the given pattern and then uses it to efficiently search for the pattern within the text. It iterates through the text using the DFA transitions until the pattern is found or the end of the text is reached. If the pattern is found, it returns the index of the first occurrence; otherwise, it returns -1.

