You are given a binary tree data structure and are required to implement a function that calculates the average value of each level in the tree. The tree is represented using a standard node structure with left and right pointers. The function should return a vector of doubles, where each element represents the average value of nodes at each level in the tree.

The node structure is defined as follows:
```cpp
struct TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode *left;
    TreeNode *right;
    TreeNode(int x) : val(x), left(NULL), right(NULL) {}
};
```

You need to implement the following function:
```cpp
vector<double> averageOfLevels(TreeNode* root);
```

The function should take the root of the binary tree as input and return a vector of doubles representing the average value of nodes at each level in the tree.

For example, given the following binary tree:
```
    3
   / \
  9  20
    /  \
   15   7
```
The function should return the vector `{3, 14.5, 11}` representing the average values of nodes at each level.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <queue>

vector<double> averageOfLevels(TreeNode* root) {
    vector<double> result;
    if (root == nullptr) {
        return result;
    }

    queue<TreeNode*> q;
    q.push(root);

    while (!q.empty()) {
        int sz = q.size();
        double sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < sz; i++) {
            TreeNode* node = q.front();
            q.pop();
            sum += node->val;
            if (node->left) {
                q.push(node->left);
            }
            if (node->right) {
                q.push(node->right);
            }
        }
        result.push_back(sum / sz);
    }

    return result;
}
```

